arly indicate imitation of a specific cultural song, making this speculative.\n    *   D) (Blank): Cannot be chosen.\n\n**Re-evaluation:** The speaker is clearly performing a comedic routine. The refusal to play with lions is the setup. The singing acts as a punchline or a transition. Since the speaker is actively performing and using vocal variety (shouting, strong language, then singing), the most plausible general reason for inserting a song into a monologue, especially when other specific reasons (A and C) are poorly supported by the immediate context, is to demonstrate a facet of his performance—his musicality or ability to switch vocal styles (B). The song serves as a final, slightly absurd punctuation mark to his refusal.\n\nGiven the options, and recognizing this is a performance, B is the most general fit for why someone would suddenly sing in the middle of a speech, even if the song itself is contextually tied to the preceding statement.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{B}$
